      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the ratio of the height of the shorter cone to the original height. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the radius of the base of the original cone.\nThe circumference of the base of the original cone is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. We can find the radius \\(r\\) using the formula for the circumference of a circle:\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi\n\\]\nDividing both sides by \\(2\\pi\\), we get:\n\\[\nr = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the volume of the original cone.\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nFor the original cone:\n\\[\nr = 8 \\text{ inches}, \\quad h = 30 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\\[\nV_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30)\n\\]\n\\[\nV_{\\text{original}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) (30)\n\\]\n\\[\nV_{\\text{original}} = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the height of the shorter cone.\nWe know that the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches and the radius of the base remains \\(8\\) inches. Using the volume formula for the shorter cone:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{1}{3} (64) h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{64}{3} h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\\[\n576 = 64 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\nSolving for \\(h_{\\text{shorter}}\\):\n\\[\nh_{\\text{shorter}} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Calculate the ratio of the shorter height to the original height.\nThe original height is \\(30\\) inches and the shorter height is \\(9\\) inches. The ratio is:\n\\[\n\\frac{h_{\\text{shorter}}}{h_{\\text{original}}} = \\frac{9}{30}\n\\]\nSimplifying the fraction:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\\).",
