      "text": "The volume of a cone is given by the formula $V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$, where $r$ is the radius of the base and $h$ is the height of the cone. We are given the circumference of the base as $16\\pi$ inches, so the radius is $\\frac{16}{2} = 8$ inches. The volume of the original cone is $\\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2) (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi 64 \\cdot 30 = 640\\pi$ cubic inches. \n\nNow, the height of the shorter cone is reduced while the circumference stays the same, so the new height is $\\frac{24}{30} = \\frac{4}{5}$ times the original height. The radius of the base remains the same, so the new volume of the cone is $\\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2) (\\frac{4}{5}(30)) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi 64 \\cdot \\frac{48}{5} = \\frac{3072}{15} \\pi$ cubic inches. We are given that this volume is $192\\pi$ cubic inches, so we can write $\\frac{3072}{15} \\pi = 192\\pi$. Dividing through by $\\pi$, we get $\\frac{3072}{15} = 192$, or $3072/15 = 192$. Multiplying both sides by 15, we get $3072 = 2904$. Dividing both sides by 2904, we get $\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$. So the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is $\\boxed{\\frac{4}{5}}$.The answer is: \\frac{4}{5}",
